    faq: [{"q": "Can Claude for Excel rebuild a real estate proforma from a PDF?", "a": "In the test described here, yes. The investor gave Claude for Excel screenshots of the sources and uses and the proforma, pasted the rent roll, and supplied the waterfall when asked. Claude produced a working model in about 7 to 10 minutes. She spot-checked a few formulas and estimated a full check at a couple of hours, so treat the output as a draft."}, {"q": "What is Claude for Excel?", "a": "It's Anthropic's add-in that brings Claude into Excel. It can answer questions about an open workbook, adjust assumptions while keeping formula relationships intact, debug errors, and build or populate models. Anthropic lists it for Pro, Max, Team, and Enterprise plans on Excel for the web, Windows, and Mac."}, {"q": "Can Claude for Excel model a real estate equity waterfall?", "a": "In this example, Claude asked for the waterfall terms before building it, and the investor provided one more screenshot. Check each tier, hurdle, and catch-up against the operating agreement, not just the investor deck."}, {"q": "Should I trust a real estate proforma built in Claude for Excel?", "a": "Not blindly. The creator of this workflow said so directly. Use it to see a sponsor's assumptions quickly, then verify the formulas, inputs, and rent roll before you rely on the numbers. Anthropic also cautions against using Claude for Excel for final deliverables or audit-critical calculations without human review."}, {"q": "Why rebuild a sponsor's real estate proforma at all?", "a": "Rebuilding a model the sponsor calls proprietary shows you which assumptions drive the returns, such as rent growth, exit cap rate, and how distributions split in the waterfall, so you can pressure-test them before you invest."}],
